# gpgme -- the library flatpak actually links, and the end of the GnuPG chain.
#
# flatpak's meson has dependency('gpgme', version: '>=1.8.0') with NO OPTION
# GUARD. This is the single line that made six other recipes necessary, because
# gpgme does not implement crypto: it SPAWNS gpg and drives it over Assuan. So
# the chain is libgpg-error -> libassuan/libgcrypt/libksba/npth -> gnupg -> here.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://gnupg.org/ftp/gcrypt/gpgme/gpgme-2.0.0.tar.bz2
SRC_SHA256=ddf161d3c41ff6a3fcbaf4be6c6e305ca4ef1cc3f1ecdfce0c8c2a167c0cc36d
SRC_DIR=gpgme-2.0.0

# --enable-languages= (EMPTY) IS THE PAYLOAD DEFAULT AGAIN, AND IT IS THE SECOND
# TIME THE SAME BINDING HAS APPEARED IN THIS CHAIN.
#
# configure sets default_languages="cl" and, when the flag is absent, resolves
# enabled_languages to that default -- so a Common Lisp binding is built and
# INSTALLED unless it is switched off. libgpg-error does the identical thing
# through --disable-languages. Two of the seven GnuPG recipes ship a Lisp binding
# by default, which makes it a property of how this upstream packages rather than
# a quirk of one component.
#
# The empty value is the documented way off: configure reads
#     if test "x$enabled_languages" = "x" -o "$enabled_languages" = "no"
#     then enabled_languages=
# so "=" and "=no" both mean none. Passing it explicitly also flips
# explicit_languages=1, which turns a missing binding requirement from a WARNING
# into an ERROR -- so if a future gpgme adds a default binding this recipe cannot
# satisfy, the build stops rather than quietly shipping less than it claims.
#
# --disable-gpgsm-test and --disable-g13-test. gnupg here is built
# --disable-gpgsm and without g13, so those programs do not exist. configure
# otherwise probes for them and warns; the tests they gate are not run by this
# recipe either way. Named so the absence is a decision that FOLLOWS FROM the
# gnupg recipe rather than an unexplained pair of flags -- if gpgsm is ever
# enabled there, these two come off together.
#
# --disable-static restates libtool's default, kept for consistency with the rest
# of the chain and because that default is libtool's rather than this package's.
#
# NOT PASSED, AND CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED: --enable-fixed-path. It would bake
# an absolute search path for gpg into the library. Without it configure leaves
# fixed_search_path unset and gpgme LOOKS UP gpg ON $PATH AT RUN TIME, which is
# what this distribution wants -- the build seeds dependencies under /deps and
# the runtime has them under /usr, so a path captured at configure time would be
# a path that is correct in the builder and wrong on the installed system. That
# is the shape of a defect that would appear only after installation, so it was
# worth confirming the default is the safe one rather than assuming it.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--disable-static --enable-languages= \
--disable-gpgsm-test --disable-g13-test"
